2026-07-23T09:18:27.415-05:00	feedback	row=0000	actual_labels.jsonl	reviewer_note	Negative and positive functional coverage exists through TC-HC-010-A and TC-HC-010-B. Boundary/limit testing is covered by TC-HC-010-C through evaluation of the time limits.	by=dsobc
2026-07-23T09:18:27.415-05:00	feedback	row=0003	actual_labels.jsonl	reviewer_note	Positive and negative tests are covered. In addition, so is the timepoint boundary.	by=dsobc
2026-07-23T09:18:27.415-05:00	feedback	row=0004	actual_labels.jsonl	reviewer_note	The immutability of the audit entry is confirmed by TC-HC-014-B.	by=dsobc
2026-07-23T09:18:27.415-05:00	feedback	row=0005	actual_labels.jsonl	reviewer_note	TC-HC-015-C was added to the dataset as an additional functional (positive) test case which checks that there could be other medications on the patient's list which (if active) would lead to a drug-drug interaction; however, the system won't flag this false positive case because it correctly detects those other medications on the patient's list as inactive.	by=dsobc
2026-07-23T09:18:27.415-05:00	feedback	row=0006	actual_labels.jsonl	reviewer_note	Overall the test cases satisfy the requirement functionality. However, it remains unclear in the requirement what is meant by "all other roles". These roles should be referenced or listed here to ensure that all the "other roles" are denied as stated in the requirement. Additionally, it needs to be clarified whether any higher access roles (such as system admin) would be able to access the note data and if whether this is intended should be clarified in the requirement. Consider this revision to the requirement to ensure that all roles or role groups are tested.	by=dsobc
2026-07-23T09:18:27.415-05:00	feedback	row=0007	actual_labels.jsonl	reviewer_note	Positive, negative, and boundary tests are covered by TC-HC-017-A , TC-HC-017-B, TC-HC-017-C. The requirement specs are anticipated to include:   System shall calculate pediatric dose as milligrams per kilogram.  System shall calculate pediatric dose when a body weight value is recorded. System shall round the calculated dose to no more than one decimal place.   However, there isn't necessarily any limits defined as to the recorded weight boundary values; perhaps this is handled separately by another requirement but could be a useful clarification question. Furthermore, is the selected dose/weight only available in units of mg and kg? If not, this would trigger additional tests and edge case considerations (for example: could be region dependent).	by=dsobc
2026-07-23T09:18:27.415-05:00	feedback	row=0008	actual_labels.jsonl	reviewer_note	The tests cover the functional, positive, and negative aspects of the requirement. Basic decomposition of the requirement: 1. system SHALL transmit a signed prescription to the selected pharmacy 2. system SHALL transmit a signed prescription within 60 seconds of signing 3. system SHALL update the order status to 'Sent' upon receiving pharmacy confirmation	by=dsobc
2026-07-23T09:18:27.415-05:00	feedback	row=0009	actual_labels.jsonl	reviewer_note	While the basic and literal boundary coverage is intact, there are a few clarification questions worth noting:  - the reason provided for the override could be a single character repeated (still meets the requirement of >= 10 characters; there should be tests (or update to the requirement) to consider the implicit sub-requirement that the reasoning provided for the override is a coherent statement. Consider some simple text analytics tools to ensure that the override provided resembles a sentence; a more advanced solution could be testing against a LLM or similarity calculation of the provided override reason against a repository of past override reasons. This would serve as an additional check that not only does the override meet the said length requirement but is likely a valid reason.	by=dsobc
2026-07-23T09:18:27.415-05:00	feedback	row=0010	actual_labels.jsonl	reviewer_note	Agreed because there is no test that confirms the flagging of the resulting order as 'Verbal Order Pending Co-Sign'. Additionally, there is not positive test that confirms the pharmacist role can even perform the happy path workflow correctly. Additional notes: It would be helpful to include either in the requirement of the test cases setup, what are the roles? This type of feedback may be most appropriate as a consideration within the requirement to list all the roles that should be tested rather than just state "user without the Pharmacist role". If this isn't defined, this could be a clarification question that is available in the final output (not necessarily a change to the input dataset). Additionally, per the design docs, it mentions that the pharmacist marks the order manually as "Verbal Order Pending Co-Sign". Another (potential) edge case not covered here is, in the event the order is converted to electronic and the pharmacist doesn't manually flag it, what happens? Is this a scenario that should even be allowed?	by=dsobc
2026-07-23T09:18:27.415-05:00	feedback	row=0011	actual_labels.jsonl	reviewer_note	No tests to handle already existent MRNs. Furthermore there is no functional test to confirm the presence of the conflicting MRN message upon rejection.  Additional notes: Is MRN the only field that gates patient registration? Consider, what if all the same patient information but the MRN is unique? Is that allowed? Could be unforeseen edge case.	by=dsobc
2026-07-23T09:18:27.415-05:00	feedback	row=0012	actual_labels.jsonl	reviewer_note	To properly test this requirement, there needs to be a consideration that the sepsis alert is triggered iff: - A) heart rate > 90 bpm - B) temperature > 38.0 celsius - both A) and B) both occurred within a 4-hour window  Thus, there should be negative tests to confirm: - no sepsis alert occurs if only heart heart is exceeded - no sepsis alert occurs if only temperature is exceeded - no sepsis alert if heart is exceeded and then at 4 hours + 1 minute (depends on detectability limits as this could be even at the seconds level potentially)   Edge cases:  - no testing around the exact limit of 4 hours. - detectability of the temperature/heart rate is unclear - if the temperature exceeds for a very brief time (say 0.5 seconds), does that count? is there a minimum length of time the temperature / bpm needs to be sustained at that high level in order for the system to record it; otherwise, there could be blips in these values that result in false positives OR if the system is unable to detect at the desired rate, there could be false negatives related to alert reporting.	by=dsobc
