2026-07-23T09:47:15.409-05:00	feedback	row=0010	actual_labels.jsonl	reviewer_note	Agreed because there is no test that confirms the flagging of the resulting order as 'Verbal Order Pending Co-Sign'. Additionally, there is not positive test that confirms the pharmacist role can even perform the happy path workflow correctly. Additional notes: It would be helpful to include either in the requirement of the test cases setup, what are the roles? This type of feedback may be most appropriate as a consideration within the requirement to list all the roles that should be tested rather than just state "user without the Pharmacist role". If this isn't defined, this could be a clarification question that is available in the final output (not necessarily a change to the input dataset). Additionally, per the design docs, it mentions that the pharmacist marks the order manually as "Verbal Order Pending Co-Sign". Another (potential) edge case not covered here is, in the event the order is converted to electronic and the pharmacist doesn't manually flag it, what happens? Is this a scenario that should even be allowed?	by=dsobc
2026-07-23T09:47:15.409-05:00	feedback	row=0011	actual_labels.jsonl	reviewer_note	No tests to handle already existent MRNs. Furthermore there is no functional test to confirm the presence of the conflicting MRN message upon rejection.  Additional notes: Is MRN the only field that gates patient registration? Consider, what if all the same patient information but the MRN is unique? Is that allowed? Could be unforeseen edge case.	by=dsobc
2026-07-23T09:47:15.409-05:00	feedback	row=0012	actual_labels.jsonl	reviewer_note	To properly test this requirement, there needs to be a consideration that the sepsis alert is triggered iff: - A) heart rate > 90 bpm - B) temperature > 38.0 celsius - both A) and B) both occurred within a 4-hour window  Thus, there should be negative tests to confirm: - no sepsis alert occurs if only heart heart is exceeded - no sepsis alert occurs if only temperature is exceeded - no sepsis alert if heart is exceeded and then at 4 hours + 1 minute (depends on detectability limits as this could be even at the seconds level potentially)   Edge cases:  - no testing around the exact limit of 4 hours. - detectability of the temperature/heart rate is unclear - if the temperature exceeds for a very brief time (say 0.5 seconds), does that count? is there a minimum length of time the temperature / bpm needs to be sustained at that high level in order for the system to record it; otherwise, there could be blips in these values that result in false positives OR if the system is unable to detect at the desired rate, there could be false negatives related to alert reporting.	by=dsobc
2026-07-23T09:47:15.409-05:00	feedback	row=0013	actual_labels.jsonl	reviewer_note	This requirement contains several sub-requirements:  - The system SHALL allow a provider to place a laboratory order - The system SHALL allow selection of a collection priority of STAT  - The system SHALL allow selection of a collection priority of Routine - The system SHALL print a specimen label containing the patient's MRN - The system SHALL print a specimen label containing the laboratory order ID.  However, the tests only cover:   Positive test: Verify a provider can place a laboratory order with a STAT  Negative test (general): Verify a laboratory order cannot be signed without a collection priority selected.  There are additional negative/positive tests that apply based on the different sub-requirements identified.	by=dsobc
2026-07-23T09:47:15.409-05:00	feedback	row=0014	actual_labels.jsonl	reviewer_note	This requirement looks to specifically test a unique scenario where a patient with an infection control entry is displayed with a banner. The tests do not match the terminology used (should be "Isolation Precautions") not quarantine.  It is acceptable to state that negative tests are "N-A" because It is clarified in the requirement "Context:" that non-active contact isolation orders patients is covered elsewhere (e.g., in a different requirement).	by=dsobc
2026-07-23T09:47:15.410-05:00	feedback	row=0015	actual_labels.jsonl	reviewer_note	Mostly Agree: There are no negative tests which cover that one-factor authentication isn't sufficient.   Additional notes: Also, what about a scenario where 1-factor is used for an internal IP address? Shouldn't that be covered here? If nothing else, there should be a clarification question. If internal IP addresses are not covered here, it should be covered elsewhere or there should be a justification as to why they don't apply, such as in a requirement Context field.	by=dsobc
2026-07-23T09:47:15.410-05:00	feedback	row=0016	actual_labels.jsonl	reviewer_note	I would expect spec coverage completion to be No in this case because the edge case around retrieval time is not tested.	by=dsobc
2026-07-23T09:47:15.410-05:00	feedback	row=0017	actual_labels.jsonl	reviewer_note	While there is partial coverage of positive/negative, it isn't complete.  - No test to confirm no block of a duplicate record beyond 30 minutes.  - No test to confirm documentation of the administration time. - No test to confirm that roles other than nurse behave differently  SHALL let a nurse document a medication administration SHALL capture the administration time SHALL block a duplicate administration recorded within 30 minutes of the previous one  Clarification questions and comments should speak to these points.	by=dsobc
